          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In the immunopathogenesis of allergic rhinitis, which Th2 cytokines drive B-cell</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In the immunopathogenesis of allergic rhinitis, which Th2 cytokines drive B-cell class-switch recombination to IgE production?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>IL-1 and TNF-α</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>IL-1 and TNF-α are pro-inflammatory cytokines of innate immunity. They do not drive the Th2-mediated IgE class-switch that underlies allergic rhinitis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>IL-4 and IL-13</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. IL-4 and IL-13, produced by Th2 cells, instruct B-cells to undergo class-switch recombination from IgM/IgG to IgE, which then binds FcεRI receptors on mucosal mast cells.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>IL-12 and IFN-γ</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>IL-12 and IFN-γ are Th1 cytokines that actually suppress IgE production and promote a Th1 response — the opposite of the pathway driving allergic rhinitis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>IL-6 and IL-17</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>IL-6 is a pleiotropic cytokine and IL-17 is produced by Th17 cells. Neither is responsible for the Th2-driven IgE class-switch recombination in allergic rhinitis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is IL-4 and IL-13. These Th2 cytokines instruct B-cells to switch from IgM/IgG to IgE production, which arms mucosal mast cells via high-affinity FcεRI receptors."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done! IL-4 and IL-13 are the key Th2 cytokines that drive B-cell class-switch recombination to IgE — the central immunological event in allergic rhinitis."
                }
              ],
