          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which integrated parameters are used to classify the severity of an acute asthma</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which integrated parameters are used to classify the severity of an acute asthma exacerbation and determine the immediate management pathway in a child?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Serum IgE level, eosinophil count, and chest X-ray findings</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>These investigations are useful for diagnosing atopic asthma and assessing chronic disease status but are not the bedside parameters for rapid classification of acute exacerbation severity.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Skin prick test results, spirometry, and blood eosinophils</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Skin prick testing and spirometry are diagnostic tools for chronic asthma assessment, not for the rapid classification of an acute exacerbation requiring emergency management.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Clinical signs, SpO₂, and PEFR</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Acute exacerbation severity is classified using an integrated assessment of clinical signs (wheeze, accessory muscle use, ability to speak), oxygen saturation (SpO₂), and peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR).</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Arterial blood gas analysis alone</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Arterial blood gas may be needed in severe or life-threatening exacerbations but is not the initial integrated assessment tool for classifying severity at presentation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is clinical signs, SpO₂, and PEFR. This integrated bedside assessment classifies exacerbation severity and guides the immediate management pathway — a time-critical decision in paediatric emergency medicine."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Excellent. The combination of clinical signs, SpO₂, and PEFR allows rapid bedside classification of exacerbation severity and guides time-critical management decisions."
                }
              ],
