          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>What does the evidence show about the clinical impact of correcting inhaler tech</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>What does the evidence show about the clinical impact of correcting inhaler technique in children with inadequately controlled asthma?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>It has minimal impact if the child is already on appropriate medication</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This is a common misconception. Technique failure — not medication failure — is the most common cause of poor asthma control, with up to 80% of children using inhalers incorrectly.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It improves asthma control equivalently to stepping up medication</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Studies show that correcting technique alone improves asthma control equivalently to a step-up in medication, because technique failure is the most common cause of inadequate control.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It is beneficial only for children using nebulisers</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Technique matters across all device types. The pMDI with spacer — the most important device for children — is where incorrect use most commonly drives treatment failure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It is less effective than switching from inhaled to oral therapy</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Inhaled therapy remains the cornerstone of asthma management. The issue is not the route but the technique — correcting it is as powerful as escalating medication.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is that correcting technique improves control equivalently to stepping up medication. Technique failure — not medication failure — is the single most common cause of poor asthma control in children."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. Correcting inhaler technique is as powerful as stepping up medication — a critical insight, given that up to 80% of children and their carers use devices incorrectly."
                }
              ],
